From: custom
Subject: [PATCH] mt7981 cudy tr3000 256mb: pin the factory ubi partition to 235520k

The "default" layout let the UBI partition size fall out of `-(ubi)`:

    mtdparts = "...2048k(fip),-(ubi)";

`-(ubi)` expands to whatever the NMBM device has left, and that depends on
how many blocks *this* U-Boot reserved for NMBM. The factory bootloader and
the factory device tree both use a fixed 235520 KiB:

  * the only mtd literal inside the factory FIP is

        nmbm0:1024k(bl2),512k(u-boot-env),2048k(factory),256k(bdinfo),2048k(fip),235520k(ubi)

  * the factory kernel device tree, taken out of the factory UBI, has

        partition@5C0000 { label = "ubi"; reg = <0x5c0000 0xe600000>; }

    0xe600000 = 235520 KiB, and the five partitions below it line up
    byte for byte with the U-Boot side.

This U-Boot reserves fewer NMBM blocks than the factory one, so `-(ubi)`
lands a few MiB *above* 235520 KiB. U-Boot then attaches the UBI over a
range reaching past the end of the real UBI, adopts the blank trailing
erase blocks as free space, and relocates the volume table. The kernel
still sees a 235520 KiB partition from its own device tree and can no
longer find that table:

    ubi0 error: ubi_read_volume_table: the layout volume was not found
    ubi0 error: ubi_attach_mtd_dev: failed to attach mtd6, error -22
    Kernel panic - not syncing: VFS: Unable to mount root fs
    Rebooting in 1 seconds..

which is exactly the boot loop this board shows. It is also why rewriting
the firmware does not help: the bootloader keeps re-adopting the same
oversized range.

Spell the size out. 235520k is what the factory mtdparts says, what the
factory device tree says and what the UBI on the flash was created with;
the remaining bytes up to the end of nmbm0 are simply left unclaimed.

The 112m layout already uses an explicit size and is untouched.

Requires 0001 .. 0005.

Apply with:
    git apply 0006-uboot-pin-factory-ubi-size.patch
Fallback if your tree normalises EOLs differently:
    git apply --ignore-whitespace 0006-uboot-pin-factory-ubi-size.patch
    patch -p1 -l < 0006-uboot-pin-factory-ubi-size.patch

---
diff --git a/uboot-mtk-20250711/arch/arm/dts/mt7981-cudy-tr3000-v1-256mb.dts b/uboot-mtk-20250711/arch/arm/dts/mt7981-cudy-tr3000-v1-256mb.dts
index f91c170..88bc314 100644
--- a/uboot-mtk-20250711/arch/arm/dts/mt7981-cudy-tr3000-v1-256mb.dts
+++ b/uboot-mtk-20250711/arch/arm/dts/mt7981-cudy-tr3000-v1-256mb.dts
@@ -56,14 +56,31 @@
 		 * layout@0 -- the factory map, and the map the OpenWrt /
 		 * ImmortalWrt images for this board are built against.
 		 *
-		 * `-(ubi)` expands to "everything the NMBM device has left", which
-		 * is the 235520 KiB the factory mtdparts string spells out
-		 * explicitly.
+		 * The UBI size is spelled out instead of left to `-(ubi)`, and
+		 * that matters more than it looks.
+		 *
+		 * `-(ubi)` means "everything the NMBM device has left", and how
+		 * much that is depends on how many blocks *this* bootloader
+		 * reserved for NMBM -- not on how many the factory one reserved.
+		 * The two do not agree here, so `-(ubi)` comes out larger than the
+		 * 235520 KiB the factory bootloader and the factory device tree
+		 * both use. U-Boot would then attach the UBI over a range running
+		 * past the end of the real UBI, take the blank trailing erase
+		 * blocks for free space and relocate the volume table -- after
+		 * which the kernel, which still sees a 235520 KiB partition in its
+		 * own device tree, cannot find it any more:
+		 *
+		 *     ubi0 error: ubi_read_volume_table: the layout volume was not found
+		 *     ubi0 error: ubi_attach_mtd_dev: failed to attach mtd6, error -22
+		 *     Kernel panic - not syncing: VFS: Unable to mount root fs
+		 *
+		 * So pin the partition to the factory size and leave the slack
+		 * between it and the end of nmbm0 unclaimed.
 		 */
 		layout@0 {
 			label = "default";
 			mtdids = "nmbm0=nmbm0";
-			mtdparts = "nmbm0:1024k(bl2),512k(u-boot-env),2048k(Factory),256k(bdinfo),2048k(fip),-(ubi)";
+			mtdparts = "nmbm0:1024k(bl2),512k(u-boot-env),2048k(Factory),256k(bdinfo),2048k(fip),235520k(ubi)";
 		};
 
 		/*
